<div class="release-key-findings">
    <h3 class="pub-findings-header">Key Findings</h3>

    <ul class="pub-findings-list">

        <li><p>The Consumer Price Index (CPI) rose by 3.4% between July 2025 and July 2026. <span>This 3.4% increase was the same level of year-on-year inflation that was recorded in June 2026.</span></p></li>

        <li><p>Excluding Energy and Unprocessed Food, the CPI grew by 3.2% in the 12 months to July 2026.</p></li>

        <li><p>The divisions with the largest increases in the 12 months to July 2026 were Education Services (+8.9%), Clothing &amp; Footwear (+8.5%), Housing, Water, Electricity, Gas &amp; Other Fuels (+7.7%) and Insurance &amp; Financial Services (+5.5%).</p></li>

        <li><p>No division recorded a decline in prices when compared with July 2025, the first time this has occurred since December 2021.&nbsp;</p></li>

        <li><p>Consumer prices rose by 0.1% in the month between June 2026 and July 2026.</p></li>

        <li><p>The divisions that recorded the highest rises in the month were Recreation, Sport &amp; Culture (+2.7%) and Housing, Water, Electricity, Gas &amp; Other Fuels (+0.8%).</p></li>

        <li><p>The divisions that recorded the largest decreases in the month were Clothing &amp; Footwear (-5.1%) and Transport (-0.9%).</p></li>

        

    </ul>
</div>

    <div class="pub-comment">
        <h3>Statistician's Comment</h3>
        <p>The Central Statistics Office (CSO) has today (13 August 2026) published the Consumer Price Index (CPI) July 2026.</p>
<p>Commenting on the release, Edel Flannery, Senior Statistician in the Prices Division, said:<em> </em>&ldquo;<em>Today&rsquo;s release of the CPI shows that prices for consumer goods and services in July 2026 rose by 3.4% on average when compared with July 2025.</em></p>
<h4>Annual Changes&nbsp;</h4>
<p><em>The most significant increases in the 12 months to July 2026 were seen in</em><em> </em><em>Education Services (+8.9%) and Clothing &amp; Footwear (+8.5%)</em><em>. The annual change in Education Services reflects a rise in costs associated with third-level education which came into effect from October 2025. The rise in Clothing &amp; Footwear was due to higher costs for garments, shoes and other footwear</em><em>. No division recorded an overall decline in prices when compared with July 2025.</em></p>
<h4>Monthly Changes</h4>
<p><em>Consumer prices in July 2026 rose by 0.1% in the month. During July 2025, prices also increased by 0.1% in the month. In July 2026, the most significant </em><em>monthly increases were in Recreation, Sport &amp; Culture (+2.7%) and Housing, Water, Electricity, Gas &amp; Other Fuels (+0.8%). </em><em>The rise in Recreation, Sport &amp; Culture prices was primarily due to higher costs for package holidays. Housing, Water, Electricity, Gas &amp; Other Fuels rose mainly due to higher costs for electricity and mortgage interest. This was partially offset by a decrease in the cost of home heating oil.</em></p>
<p><em>The largest </em><em>monthly decreases </em><em>were in Clothing &amp; Footwear (-5.1</em><em>%</em><em>) and Transport (-0.9%).</em></p>
<p><em>It should be noted that these figures relate to the CPI. The <a href="/en/csolatestnews/pressreleases/2026pressreleases/pressstatementflashestimatefortheharmonisedindexofconsumerpricesjuly2026/">Flash Estimate Press Statement</a> published</em><em> on 30 July 2026 related to the EU Harmonised Index of Consumer Prices (HICP). </em><em>See <a href="/en/releasesandpublications/ep/p-cpi/consumerpriceindexjuly2026/backgroundnotes/">Background Notes</a> for differences between CPI and HICP.</em><em>"</em></p>
<h4>National Average Prices&nbsp;&nbsp;</h4>
<p>The National Average Prices for selected goods and services for July 2026 are also published today. The National Average Prices are compiled together with the CPI.</p>
<p>Commenting on the National Average Prices, Edel Flannery said: <em>&ldquo;There were price decreases in the 12 months to July 2026 for a pound of butter (-55c), a 2.5kg bag of potatoes, 2 litres of full fat milk, Irish cheddar per kg (all -9c), sirloin steak per kg (-5c) and an 800g loaf of brown sliced pan (-2c). The price of an 800g loaf of white sliced pan was unchanged when compared with July 2025.</em></p>
<p><em>The national average price for a litre of diesel in July 2026 was &euro;1.76, an increase of 6c on the same time last year. Petrol prices increased by 5c to &euro;1.77 a litre. It should be noted that July prices were collected in mid-July and may not reflect more recent increases in the cost of diesel, petrol and home heating oil resulting from the ongoing situation in the Middle East."</em></p>
<p>The comprehensive list of items included in the National Average Prices and their monthly prices are available in <a href="https://data.cso.ie/table/CPM12">PxStat Table CPM12</a>.</p>
    </div>


